WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.799
Into The Woods is an adaptation of Stephen
Sondheim &amp; James Lapine’s Tony Award-winning

00:00:04.799 --> 00:00:09.710
stage musical which blends a bunch of Brothers
Grimm fairy tales and throws an original story

00:00:09.710 --> 00:00:11.110
into the mix for good measure.

00:00:11.110 --> 00:00:15.099
The classic fairy tales that get a modern
twist are Cinderella, Jack And The Beanstalk,

00:00:15.099 --> 00:00:17.010
Little Red Riding Hood, and Rapunzel.

00:00:17.010 --> 00:00:20.760
While the new story that links them all together
follows a childless Baker and his Wife as

00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:24.189
they try to reverse a curse placed on them
by a wicked witch.

00:00:24.189 --> 00:00:28.580
I love a good musical – whether that’s
classics like Singin’ In The Rain, 7 Brides

00:00:28.580 --> 00:00:32.770
For 7 Brothers, Calamity Jane, Meet Me In
St Louis and many more; or modern movies like

00:00:32.770 --> 00:00:35.900
Chicago, Les Mis, Enchanted, Once or Pitch
Perfect.

00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:41.210
This isn’t director Rob Marshall’s first
all-star musical, though his success rate’s

00:00:41.210 --> 00:00:43.390
been a somewhat hit and miss.

00:00:43.390 --> 00:00:47.850
There was the brilliant Oscar-winning movie
adaptation of Kander &amp; Ebb’s Chicago with

00:00:47.850 --> 00:00:50.940
Catherine Zeta Jones, Renee Zellweger and
Richard Geer.

00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:56.360
Then there was also the not-quite-so brilliant
Nine, which was even more star-studded and

00:00:56.360 --> 00:01:00.380
featured Daniel Day-Lewis, Kate Hudson, Judi
Dench, Marion Cotillard, Sophia Loren, Nicole

00:01:00.380 --> 00:01:03.330
Kidman, Penelope Cruz, and Fergie from the
Black-Eyed Peas.

00:01:03.330 --> 00:01:08.170
But Marshall’s musical experience doesn’t
end there: he won a Primetime Emmy for his

00:01:08.170 --> 00:01:12.710
choreography on the TV movie of Annie, which
he also directed; and he’s choreographed

00:01:12.710 --> 00:01:17.320
movie musicals including Victor/Victoria with
Julie Andrews, and stage musicals such as

00:01:17.320 --> 00:01:18.320
Cabaret.

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:20.990
So, basically, he’s got movie musical form!

00:01:20.990 --> 00:01:25.680
I've not seen the original stage version but
it’s been rather controversial that Disney

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:29.750
was adapting this musical, especially in terms
of what they’d cut and whether the musical’s

00:01:29.750 --> 00:01:31.310
tone would suffer.

00:01:31.310 --> 00:01:37.450
It’s already getting awards nominations,
including the Golden Globes.

00:01:37.450 --> 00:01:42.280
The movie gets you right into the fairy-tale
mood from the start, as it kicks off with

00:01:42.280 --> 00:01:45.060
the line ‘Once upon a time, in a far-off
kingdom….’

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:48.530
The line’s spoken by an unseen James Corden,
who plays The Baker.

00:01:48.530 --> 00:01:53.540
We hear his narration here and there throughout
the film, though the voiceover’s never over-bearing

00:01:53.540 --> 00:01:56.880
as it’s used sparingly and fits neatly with
the fairy-tale setting.

00:01:56.880 --> 00:02:02.600
The Brothers Grimm fairy tales and Sondheim’s
musical are both pretty dark, and although

00:02:02.600 --> 00:02:07.620
this is a PG movie from Disney, it still manages
to stay on the dark-ish side.

00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:11.430
Ok, so, it’s not Tim Burton’s Sweeney
Todd, which had an 18-certificate in the UK

00:02:11.430 --> 00:02:13.190
and an R rating in the US.

00:02:13.190 --> 00:02:16.480
And I kind of wonder what Tim Burton would
have done with this material.

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:21.420
Anyway, in this Into The Woods, you don’t
actually see any violence or bloodshed on

00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:25.280
screen, with the camera cutting away before
anything nasty happens.

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:30.750
But while things aren’t necessarily shown
on screen, they’re not left totally to our

00:02:30.750 --> 00:02:33.790
imagination either as it’s pretty clear
what’s going to happen.

00:02:33.790 --> 00:02:37.850
So, although the violence doesn’t feature
explicitly on screen, the movie still feels

00:02:37.850 --> 00:02:45.960
suitably dark.

00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:50.450
Mixing together characters from separate fairy
tales makes for some intriguing interactions

00:02:50.450 --> 00:02:54.950
and some lovely comic moments too, with some
particular stand-out parts in early scenes

00:02:54.950 --> 00:02:57.350
between Little Red Riding Hood and The Baker
in the woods.

00:02:57.350 --> 00:03:01.570
The only characters I felt were a bit on the
bland side were Rapunzel and her Prince.

00:03:01.570 --> 00:03:05.971
Neither’s given an awful lot of screen time
and neither has much to do when they are on

00:03:05.971 --> 00:03:07.240
screen.

00:03:07.240 --> 00:03:10.680
And there’s a thread in Rapunzel’s story
that’s kind of just left hanging.

00:03:10.680 --> 00:03:15.060
Having said that, Rapunzel’s Prince does
have a very funny scene with his brother Prince

00:03:15.060 --> 00:03:20.410
Charming, and he gets to deliver a nicely
comic line to Rapunzel a little over half-way

00:03:20.410 --> 00:03:21.410
through the movie.

00:03:21.410 --> 00:03:26.320
As inventive and witty and clever as Stephen
Sondheim’s songs are in Into The Woods,

00:03:26.320 --> 00:03:30.830
they very much exist within the setting of
the musical.

00:03:30.830 --> 00:03:34.379
I mean that they’re not really the kind
of tunes you’ll find yourself leaving the

00:03:34.379 --> 00:03:36.709
cinema singing, whistling or humming as you
go.

00:03:36.709 --> 00:03:40.880
As for the look of the movie, sets, costumes
and most of the effects, I really can’t

00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:44.120
fault the behind-the-scenes crew!

00:03:44.120 --> 00:03:47.569
Into The Woods certainly boasts a star-studded
cast!

00:03:47.569 --> 00:03:50.420
Meryl Streep is just fabulous as The Witch.

00:03:50.420 --> 00:03:55.959
That she can sing is no surprise now as we’ve
seen her sing on screen in the full-on musical

00:03:55.959 --> 00:03:58.290
Mamma Mia!, Postcards From The Edge (‘You
Don’t Know Me’), and in Robert Altman’s

00:03:58.290 --> 00:04:00.239
A Prairie Home Companion.

00:04:00.239 --> 00:04:05.680
Just as you’d expect from actress of Streep’s
calibre, she really makes you believe her

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:09.700
witch – she acts, sounds and looks the part.

00:04:09.700 --> 00:04:16.160
Costume &amp; make-up look incredible – bluey-grey,
matted hair, crooked blackened teeth, stooped/bent

00:04:16.160 --> 00:04:22.819
over, thick long yellowed fingernails, &amp; an
ashen face covered in wrinkles.

00:04:22.819 --> 00:04:29.169
And she's been nominated for Best Supporting
Actress in a Musical or Comedy at Golden Globes.

00:04:29.169 --> 00:04:32.520
Anna Kendrick’s really got the musical mania,
hasn’t she?!

00:04:32.520 --> 00:04:37.339
What with movie musicals Pitch Perfect 2 and
The Last Five Years both out in 2015, that

00:04:37.339 --> 00:04:39.930
woman just can’t stop singing!

00:04:39.930 --> 00:04:42.689
And she really hits the right note as Cinderella.

00:04:42.689 --> 00:04:47.900
Lovely attention to detail on her costumes,
whether she’s in her scrubbing-the-floor

00:04:47.900 --> 00:04:51.449
outfit or kitted out in a golden dress for
the King’s Festival.

00:04:51.449 --> 00:04:55.900
Chris Pine gives a cracking performance as
Cinderella’s Prince, really capturing his

00:04:55.900 --> 00:04:58.380
vanity and arrogance in a lovely comic turn.

00:04:58.380 --> 00:05:02.130
He’s already flexed his comedic muscles
recently in Horrible Bosses 2 where he put

00:05:02.130 --> 00:05:04.550
on an hilariously unhinged show.

00:05:04.550 --> 00:05:09.059
And he gets to develop that in Into The Woods,
with some lovely scenes with his brother who’s

00:05:09.059 --> 00:05:10.319
simply called Rapunzel’s Prince.

00:05:10.319 --> 00:05:15.680
Look out, in particular, for the song “Agony”
where they desperately try to outdo each other!

00:05:15.680 --> 00:05:20.610
Emily Blunt reunites with Her Devil Wears
Prada co-star Meryl Streep in a rather different

00:05:20.610 --> 00:05:24.169
role though she’s still treated pretty poorly
by Streep’s character!

00:05:24.169 --> 00:05:28.189
She plays The Baker’s Wife, who’s dying
to have a baby, but hasn’t been able to.

00:05:28.189 --> 00:05:32.020
If you’ve seen Blunt in anything from Looper
to The Five-Year Engagement or The Young Victoria,

00:05:32.020 --> 00:05:35.659
you’ll know she’s a fantastic actress,
but did you also know she can sing?

00:05:35.659 --> 00:05:38.419
Well, she can, and very well too; thank you
very much!

00:05:38.419 --> 00:05:42.380
In fact, this isn’t her first time belting
out a song on screen – she actually won

00:05:42.380 --> 00:05:46.550
a Golden Globe for her role in a BBC drama
called Gideon’s Daughter, where she also

00:05:46.550 --> 00:05:48.150
has to carry a tune.

00:05:48.150 --> 00:05:52.369
Anyway, I love her in Into The Woods for the
same reason I love her in just about any role

00:05:52.369 --> 00:05:55.719
– you always believe her in whatever she’s
playing.

00:05:55.719 --> 00:05:59.550
Nomd for Best Actress in a Musical or Comedy
at Golden Globes.

00:05:59.550 --> 00:06:03.360
James Corden is great as The Baker, portraying
his character with ease.

00:06:03.360 --> 00:06:06.710
You may know him for writing and starring
in hit British comedy Gavin &amp; Stacey.

00:06:06.710 --> 00:06:11.120
And, of course, he’s going to be replacing
Craig Ferguson as host of The Late Late Show

00:06:11.120 --> 00:06:12.120
in 2015!

00:06:12.120 --> 00:06:15.960
Again, this isn’t his first musical venture
– he starred as amateur opera singer and

00:06:15.960 --> 00:06:19.279
Britain’s Got Talent winner, Paul Potts
in the movie One Chance; and going back to

00:06:19.279 --> 00:06:24.639
the late 90s, he played the lead singer in
a boy band in the TV comedy Boyz Unlimited,

00:06:24.639 --> 00:06:27.780
which was co-created by David Walliams and
Matt Lucas.

00:06:27.780 --> 00:06:30.939
Johnny Depp puts in a brief appearance as
the lascivious Wolf.

00:06:30.939 --> 00:06:34.580
Depp’s no stranger to Sondheim musicals,
having starred in Tim Burton’s movie version

00:06:34.580 --> 00:06:37.649
of Sweeney Todd: The Demon Barber Of Fleet
Street.

00:06:37.649 --> 00:06:41.639
And he’s also worked with Into The Woods
director Rob Marshall before, on the 4th Pirates

00:06:41.639 --> 00:06:43.259
Of The Caribbean movie, On Stranger Tides.

00:06:43.259 --> 00:06:45.210
But I’ll try not to hold that against him!

00:06:45.210 --> 00:06:49.029
I love the understated way they’ve styled
his wolf – he’s got a fedora hat with

00:06:49.029 --> 00:06:55.149
little ears poking out, a 1940s-inspired grey-blue
furry jacket over a similarly-coloured shirt

00:06:55.149 --> 00:06:58.419
and tie, bushy eyebrows and moustache, and
a tail!

00:06:58.419 --> 00:07:02.599
I love the matter-of-fact interaction between
his Wolf and Red Riding Hood – it’s all

00:07:02.599 --> 00:07:06.279
so perfectly natural when they meet, ‘Good
day, young lady,’ ‘Good day, Mr Wolf’,

00:07:06.279 --> 00:07:08.659
which adds to the humour.

00:07:08.659 --> 00:07:10.809
Christine Baranski plays Cinderella’s Stepmother.

00:07:10.809 --> 00:07:14.869
Again, she’s got musical form – she worked
alongside Meryl Streep in Mamma Mia!, with

00:07:14.869 --> 00:07:18.339
Into The Woods director Rob Marshall on the
movie Chicago; and she’s starred in various

00:07:18.339 --> 00:07:19.819
productions on stage.

00:07:19.819 --> 00:07:25.369
Though she doesn’t have a lot to do in the
movie, what she does she does very well.

00:07:25.369 --> 00:07:29.139
As far as Rob Marshall’s movie musicals
go, I still prefer Chicago.

00:07:29.139 --> 00:07:34.680
But Into The Woods is an enjoyably dark tale
with a sense of humour, great performances,

00:07:34.680 --> 00:07:36.879
and gorgeous sets and costumes.

00:07:36.879 --> 00:07:40.199
4 out of 5 flicks!

00:07:40.199 --> 00:07:41.970
What did you think of Into The Woods?

00:07:41.970 --> 00:07:43.449
Have you seen the stage musical?

00:07:43.449 --> 00:07:45.129
How do you think the movie compares?

00:07:45.129 --> 00:07:46.619
And what’s your favourite movie musical?

00:07:46.619 --> 00:07:49.520
Let me know in the comments below!

00:07:49.520 --> 00:07:54.330
Don’t forget to like this video &amp; subscribe
for more my weekly reviews and interviews.

00:07:54.330 --> 00:07:57.279
Thanks for watching and see ya next time.

00:07:57.279 --> 00:07:57.819
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers!

